
PRODUCT REQUIREMENTS DOCUMENT
Aggregate Working Platform
페블테크 (Pebble-Tech) Aggregate Working Platform System
Design, Construction, and Safety Requirements for the Aggregate Working Platform System
Primary Application: Ground Improvement / Working Platform Support for Building Foundations (Mat Foundations, Slab-on-Grade); Additional Application: Temporary Tracked-Plant Working Platforms in MiPM Deep Excavation and Underground Construction
Incorporating geosynthetic reinforcement (하이셀, Hi-Cell) design
Document Status: DRAFT — Rev. A
Date: 2026-08-24
Revision note (2026-08-24): Scope corrected. The primary application of this system is permanent building foundation ground improvement/support; the temporary tracked-plant working platform use (the original Rev. A framing) is an additional application, not the primary one. See Section 1.
Prepared for: TaeYang
Note: This PRD is a design/construction/safety procedure specification. It defines requirements and step-by-step procedures only — it does not implement calculation code or software.
 
1.  Purpose and Scope
This document defines the design methodology, step-by-step construction procedure, and safety requirements for the Aggregate Working Platform (페블테크) system, an engineered layer of well-graded crushed aggregate — optionally reinforced with a geocell layer (하이셀/구속셀, Hi-Cell) — placed over prepared subgrade to improve ground bearing capacity and control settlement.

The system has two applications, covered together in this PRD because they share the same materials, design-method family, and construction procedure:
(a) PRIMARY APPLICATION — permanent building foundation support: ground improvement beneath mat foundations, slab-on-grade (SOG), and isolated/strip footings for new-build structures, sized against sustained building loads and a permanent-structure settlement criterion.
(b) ADDITIONAL APPLICATION — temporary tracked-plant working platforms: a temporary structural layer supporting tracked and wheeled plant — piling rigs, cranes, excavators, and delivery vehicles — during temporary earth-retention and deep-excavation works under the MiPM underground construction system (soldier piles, waler beams, struts, rakers, diaphragm walls), sized against plant bearing pressure and a serviceability/leveling-tolerance criterion.

Both applications use the same aggregate/geocell products and the same field construction procedure (Sections 6–9); they differ in governing load case, target design method, and acceptance criterion (Section 4.2). Which application governs a given design zone must be identified explicitly before sizing — see Section 5, Step 1.
1.1 In Scope
•	Design methodology and bearing-capacity/settlement-sizing procedure for aggregate-only and geosynthetic(geocell)-reinforced ground improvement beneath permanent building foundations (primary application).
•	Design methodology and bearing-capacity/thickness-sizing procedure for aggregate-only and geosynthetic-reinforced temporary tracked-plant working platforms (additional application).
•	Step-by-step design procedure (sequence of engineering checks) for both applications — descriptive, not a computational implementation.
•	Step-by-step field construction procedure from subgrade preparation through platform/handover, common to both applications.
•	Safety measures across design, construction, and in-service (working) stages.
•	QA/QC acceptance criteria, monitoring, and maintenance during the platform's/improved ground's service life.
1.2 Out of Scope
•	Structural design of the permanent building superstructure/foundation elements themselves (this PRD covers the ground-improvement layer beneath them, not the mat/footing/slab structural design) — that is the structural engineer of record's separate deliverable.
•	Deep foundation design (e.g. 메가헬리컬파일 mega helical piles) — a different foundation system, covered under its own product specification, not this PRD.
•	Detailed structural design of the built-up I-beam sections used in MiPM temporary works themselves (covered under separate MiPM structural PRDs).
•	Software/calculation-tool implementation — this PRD intentionally specifies procedures only; no functions, scripts, or code are to be implemented from this document.
1.3 Reference Project Examples (GDS)
This PRD is intended to incorporate design and construction examples from the project reference folder (design/construction case files, referred to as "GDS"). Those files were not accessible from this working environment at the time of drafting (see Appendix C). Sections flagged “[GDS-PENDING]” should be reviewed and refined once those reference files are supplied — e.g. pasted content, uploaded files, or a shared repository.
2.  Definitions and Terminology
Korean project terminology is used throughout in parallel with English equivalents, consistent with project convention.
Term	Definition	Korean
Aggregate Working Platform	Engineered layer of well-graded crushed rock aggregate constructed over prepared subgrade to improve ground bearing capacity beneath permanent building foundations (primary application), or to distribute plant loading and provide a safe, stable operating surface for tracked/wheeled construction equipment (additional application).	페블테크 (Pebble-Tech)
Geosynthetic reinforcement	Geogrid and/or geotextile layer(s) placed at the subgrade/platform interface (or within the aggregate layer) to provide separation, filtration, lateral restraint, and tensile (membrane) reinforcement, reducing required platform thickness.	하이셀 (Hi-Cell)
Subgrade	The prepared, in-situ (or engineered fill) ground surface directly beneath the working platform.	
LSF (Load Safety Factor)	Ratio of platform+subgrade bearing resistance to applied bearing pressure from plant, per the BR470/TWf method used for the additional (temporary tracked-plant) application only (Section 4.2(b)). Target LSF ≥ 1.0 minimum; ≥ 1.25 recommended where ground data is uncertain or platform is safety-critical (e.g., piling rig outrigger position). The primary (building foundation) application uses a different acceptance basis — allowable bearing capacity (FS=3.0 on qu) and a settlement limit — not an LSF ratio; see Section 4.2(a).	
Punching shear failure	Localized bearing failure mode where plant load punches through the aggregate platform into a weaker subgrade before general (global) bearing failure develops.	
Bearing pressure (ground contact pressure)	Vertical pressure applied to the platform surface by tracked plant, crane outriggers, or wheel loads, including static, dynamic, and pitching (eccentricity) effects.	
		
		
		
		
		
