Appendix A  Appendix A — Comparative Table: Aggregate-Only vs. Geosynthetic-Reinforced Platform
Criterion	Aggregate-Only Platform	Geosynthetic-Reinforced Platform (하이셀)
Required thickness (typ.)	Baseline (governs by LSF check alone)	~20–30% thinner for equivalent LSF (Giroud–Han contribution)
Import aggregate volume / cost	Higher	Lower aggregate cost, offset by geosynthetic material + installation cost
Program / construction speed	Simple, fast to place; no laying sequence constraint	Requires careful laying/overlap sequence; slightly slower per zone but net faster where thickness reduction shortens overall earthworks
Performance on soft/variable subgrade	Requires greater thickness to compensate; more prone to punching on soft pockets	Better performance — separation prevents fines contamination; reinforcement improves punching resistance directly
Suitability for very soft subgrade (Cu very low)	May become impractical (excessive thickness)	Preferred; membrane/reinforcement action essential
Headroom-constrained sites (overhead services/structures)	Limited by required thickness	Preferred where reduced thickness is needed to fit clearance
Sensitivity to installation quality	Low — mainly compaction QC	Higher — orientation, overlap, and damage during laying materially affect performance
Removability / reuse at decommissioning	Aggregate can be reused elsewhere	Geosynthetic typically not reusable; may be left in place or disposed of
Typical application in MiPM works	Firm subgrade, short-duration platforms (excavator trafficking)	Piling rig / crane platforms on soft or variable subgrade, or headroom-constrained zones
Appendix B  Appendix B — Design Zone Record Template (for use during Section 5 execution)
The following record fields should be completed for each platform design zone; a blank template (spreadsheet or form) should be issued alongside this PRD for field/design-office use — no calculation logic is embedded in this PRD.
•	Zone ID / location reference
•	Governing plant item(s) and load case(s)
•	Subgrade characteristic Cu / CBR and source of data
•	Aggregate type and design φ'
•	Geosynthetic product and design properties (if used)
•	Calculated required thickness and resulting LSF
•	Edge distance and slope stability check result
•	Construction-stage verification method and hold-point sign-off record
